# KN-384 – Spike: Closed SR Kafka Trigger Logic (for KN-370)

**Env checked:** J2 (`SIEBEL.S_SRV_REQ`, Siebel Tools / Web Tools, Administration - Runtime Events)  
**Related:** KN-370 (Automation: SR Closed event trigger logic change)

## Context & What's Actually Happening

The ticket asks to switch the Closed SR Kafka trigger from looking at `LAST_UPD` to using `STATUS_SET_DT` instead.

After digging through the RTE config and BC User Properties, **nothing in the trigger actually checks `LAST_UPD`**. The current setup is much simpler:

* RTE on `WriteRecordUpdated` (Seq `0`) fires Action Set **`Call Event BS Async`** across 6 SR-related BCs whenever `LookupValue("WP_KAFKA_PUBLISH_SWITCH","KAFKA_SR_SWITCH")="SR_TOGGLE_ON"`.
* Inside `Call Event BS Async`, Seq `1` (`Call Event`) invokes `KafkaSendEventAsync` (`Named Method 70` -> `WP Invoke Kafka Rule Set`) with just one condition:
  ```text
  [Status] = "Closed"
  ```

Because there is no state-transition check in the RTE (and no deduplication downstream in `WP Invoke Kafka Rule Set`), any subsequent edit to an already-closed SR triggers `WriteRecordUpdated`, matches `[Status] = "Closed"`, and pushes a duplicate Kafka event. From the outside it looks like `LAST_UPD` is driving the trigger, which is where the Jira wording came from.

Also verified the field mapping: external `STATUS_SET_DT` maps to Siebel's **`SR Status Date`** (`S_SRV_REQ.SR_STAT_DT`).

---

## 1. Checking `SR_STAT_DT` (`SR Status Date`)

### 1.1 Data check on J2

I ran two queries on J2 against closed SRs where `TRUNC(LAST_UPD) > TRUNC(CREATED)` to see if `SR_STAT_DT` actually gets updated on closure and whether it carries a real timestamp (or just a date from `PreDefault = System: Today`).

**Query 1 – All historical multi-day closed SRs**

```sql
SELECT
    COUNT(*) AS TOTAL_CLOSED_M_D,
    SUM(CASE WHEN SR_STAT_DT IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS STAT_DT_IS_NULL,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TRUNC(SR_STAT_DT) = TRUNC(CREATED) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS SAME_AS_CREATED_DAY,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TRUNC(SR_STAT_DT) > TRUNC(CREATED) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS UPDATED_AFTER_CREATED,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TO_CHAR(SR_STAT_DT, 'HH24:MI:SS') <> '00:00:00' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS HAS_TIME_COMPONENT
FROM SIEBEL.S_SRV_REQ
WHERE SR_STAT_ID = 'Closed'
  AND TRUNC(LAST_UPD) > TRUNC(CREATED);
```

Results (J2):

| TOTAL_CLOSED_M_D | STAT_DT_IS_NULL | SAME_AS_CREATED_DAY | UPDATED_AFTER_CREATED | HAS_TIME_COMPONENT |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| 14,329 | 1,904 (13.3%) | 4,661 (32.5%) | 1,685 (11.8%) | 6,970 (48.6%) |

Two things stood out when checking sample rows:
1. Almost half the records (`6,970`) came back with `23:00:00` instead of `00:00:00` — that's just UK BST midnight stored in UTC, not an actual status-change timestamp. That also explains the missing `6,079` rows in the day buckets (`TRUNC(SR_STAT_DT)` ends up being `CREATED - 1 day` during summer).
2. `UPDATED_AFTER_CREATED` (`1,685`) almost matched the subset of records that also had `ACT_CLOSE_DT` populated.

To confirm that, I split the last 12 months by whether `ACT_CLOSE_DT` is populated and excluded both `00:00:00` and `23:00:00`.

**Query 2 – Last 12 months split by `ACT_CLOSE_DT` (excluding BST `23:00:00` offset)**

```sql
SELECT
    CASE WHEN ACT_CLOSE_DT IS NULL THEN 'NO_ACT_CLOSE_DT' ELSE 'HAS_ACT_CLOSE_DT' END AS CLOSE_PATH,
    COUNT(*) AS TOTAL_CLOSED,
    SUM(CASE WHEN SR_STAT_DT IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS STAT_DT_IS_NULL,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TRUNC(SR_STAT_DT) = TRUNC(CREATED) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS SAME_AS_CREATED_DAY,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TRUNC(SR_STAT_DT) > TRUNC(CREATED) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS UPDATED_AFTER_CREATED,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TO_CHAR(SR_STAT_DT, 'HH24:MI:SS') NOT IN ('00:00:00', '23:00:00') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS HAS_TIME_COMPONENT
FROM SIEBEL.S_SRV_REQ
WHERE SR_STAT_ID = 'Closed'
  AND TRUNC(LAST_UPD) > TRUNC(CREATED)
  AND CREATED >= ADD_MONTHS(SYSDATE, -12)
GROUP BY CASE WHEN ACT_CLOSE_DT IS NULL THEN 'NO_ACT_CLOSE_DT' ELSE 'HAS_ACT_CLOSE_DT' END;
```

Results (J2):

| CLOSE_PATH | TOTAL_CLOSED | STAT_DT_IS_NULL | SAME_AS_CREATED_DAY | UPDATED_AFTER_CREATED | HAS_TIME_COMPONENT |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| `HAS_ACT_CLOSE_DT` | 63 (6.5%) | 4 | 5 | 53 (84.1%) | 0 |
| `NO_ACT_CLOSE_DT` | 910 (93.5%) | 245 (26.9%) | 279 | 1 (0.1%) | 0 |

What this tells us:
* On the main closure path (`NO_ACT_CLOSE_DT`, 93.5% of closed SRs), `SR_STAT_DT` basically **never updates on close** — only 1 out of 910 rows got updated after creation. 245 are `NULL`, and the other 664 (279 same-day + 385 shifted by BST `23:00:00`) are still sitting on the creation date.
* `HAS_TIME_COMPONENT` is **0** across the board. There is no real time component anywhere in `SR_STAT_DT`.

### 1.2 Repository check (`On Field Update Set`) vs. active Kafka RTEs

Checking `Business Component User Properties` in Siebel Tools (Flat view) for anything updating `SR Status Date` returns 7 BCs. Every single one is hardcoded to `Today()` (legacy config from 2015–2017):

| BC | User Property | Value |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `Related Service Request` | `On Field Update Set 3` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |
| `WP Service Request Task` | `On Field Update Set 9` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |
| `Service Request` | `On Field Update Set 9` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |
| `WP Service Request Thin` | `On Field Update Set 9` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |
| `WP MF Service Request Thin` | `On Field Update Set 9` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |
| `WP Rest Service Request` | `On Field Update Set 9` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |
| `WP Service Request Promotion` | `On Field Update Set 9` | `"Status", "SR Status Date", "Today()"` |

Comparing that list against the **6 BCs wired to `Call Event BS Async`** (`WriteRecordUpdated`) explains the J2 numbers directly:

| BC | Wired to `Call Event BS Async` RTE? | Has `On Field Update Set` (`Today()`)? | Notes |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| `Service Request` | Yes | Yes | Date-only (`Today()`) |
| `WP Service Request Thin` | Yes | Yes | Date-only (worth verifying BC class — `CSSBusComp` ignores this UP) |
| `WP MF Service Request Thin` | Yes | Yes | Date-only (same class caveat as above) |
| `WP EAI Service Request` | **Yes** | **No** | Gap — Kafka RTE fires, `SR_STAT_DT` never updated |
| `WP Related Service Request EAI BC` | **Yes** | **No** | Gap — Kafka RTE fires, `SR_STAT_DT` never updated |
| `WP MF Related Service Request BC` | **Yes** | **No** | Gap — Kafka RTE fires, `SR_STAT_DT` never updated |
| `Related Service Request` | No | Yes | Not in Kafka RTE |
| `WP Service Request Task` | No | Yes | Not in Kafka RTE |
| `WP Rest Service Request` | No | Yes | Not in Kafka RTE |
| `WP Service Request Promotion` | No | Yes | Not in Kafka RTE |

**Why we shouldn't base the trigger on `SR_STAT_DT`:**
1. Half of the BCs firing `Call Event BS Async` (all EAI / Related ones) don't update `SR Status Date` at all. Filtering on it would drop valid closure events across ~93% of traffic.
2. Even if we added the User Prop to those 3 BCs, `Today()` has no time part — any follow-up edit on the same day would still fire a duplicate event.
3. BST stores midnight as `23:00:00` of the prior day in UTC, which creates edge-case mismatches around midnight if compared against `Today()`.
4. Switching `SR Status Date` to `Timestamp()` would require repository changes and risks breaking existing reports or downstream integrations expecting a `DTYPE_DATE` value.

